**Not by itself.** Paying $850 under protest and then receiving an adverse lease-status report would not automatically establish bad faith.

A bad-faith claim usually requires evidence that MedVance exercised contractual discretion dishonestly or to deprive you of the lease’s benefit—not merely that it took a disputed legal position. The assessment would turn on:

- whether $850 is the full amount properly due under the complete lease;
- whether MedVance accepts or rejects the payment;
- whether valid cure requires payment, notice, or another act;
- what “encumbered” means under the county’s written rule;
- exactly what MedVance reports—an accurate description of a pending dispute versus a false statement that an undisputed default remains;
- whether MedVance knows its compounded calculation conflicts with Section 8.3; and
- governing state law, including the implied covenant of good faith and fair dealing.

Evidence supporting bad faith could include MedVance knowingly reporting a cured default as uncured, falsely stating that you admitted owing $14,200, concealing your timely tender, or using an unauthorized calculation to block the inspection for an unrelated purpose. Contrary evidence would include a genuine dispute over other amounts or lease provisions and a report accurately disclosing that dispute.

Before tendering, have counsel verify the $850 calculation and the consequences of payment. A reservation such as “under protest” generally preserves arguments better than an unconditional payment, but it does not necessarily cure the alleged default, compel acceptance, or prevent termination. Send traceable payment with language such as:

> Tenant tenders $850 as the amount calculated under Section 8.3’s one-time, non-compounding late-fee provision. This tender is made under protest and with an express reservation of all rights; it does not admit that any default exists or that any additional amount is due. Please confirm receipt, application of the payment, the resulting lease status, and the exact factual statement MedVance will provide to the county.

Obtain the county’s written definition of “unencumbered lease” and ask whether documentation of payment plus a written fee dispute satisfies the inspection requirement. My recommendation is to have local counsel transmit the tender and request immediate written lease-status confirmation; do not rely on a later bad-faith claim to protect Tuesday’s inspection.

**The estoppel clause’s stated deadline controls. Sending the $850 generally does not shorten it.**

Commercial leases commonly specify a response period—often **5, 10, 15, or 20 days** after receipt—but the actual lease language and governing law determine the deadline. Check:

- whether the clause requires **the landlord** to provide a certificate, rather than only requiring the tenant to do so;
- who may request it and for what purpose;
- whether the period uses calendar or business days;
- when receipt occurs;
- required delivery method and recipient;
- whether a prescribed form must accompany the request; and
- the remedy for failing to respond.

If the clause says “promptly” or gives no deadline, the standard may be a **reasonable time under the circumstances**. That does not guarantee a response before Tuesday. Your inspection deadline provides evidence of urgency, but it does not independently rewrite the contract.

Send the tender and estoppel request separately but simultaneously, using every contractually permitted rapid method. State the Tuesday deadline and request an earlier voluntary response:

> Under Section [X], Tenant requests the required lease-status certificate. Because the county inspection is scheduled for [date and time], please provide the certificate by [earlier date and time]. Tenant is simultaneously tendering $850 under protest and with an express reservation of rights. If MedVance contends that any default remains, please identify each amount, its contractual basis, and the applicable cure deadline.

Do not state that the requested early deadline is contractually mandatory unless the clause makes it so. If the contractual response period extends beyond Tuesday, seek a voluntary expedited certificate, have counsel contact MedVance directly, and ask the county what substitute evidence it will accept. My recommendation is to follow the clause’s formal notice requirements exactly; otherwise MedVance may argue that its response period never began.
